\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The $(2,0)$ wave of the unperturbed rotating collapse. (a)~$r\,\Delta \psi _4^{20}$ of the 0.2c far-zone run on spheres $R=14$--45 against retarded time: one waveform with ring-down period ${\approx }24$; only the first lobe falls with $R$ (near field), the extrema are flat in $R$ to $1.5\%$ over $R=30$--45, and this box reproduces the $L=64$ run's throat history to $3.2\times 10^{-4}$. (b)~The wave against spin at $R=14$, each run on its own collapse clock $u=t-R-t_{10}$ ($t_{10}$: its equatorial deficit at $10\%$): the 0.30c burst has the 0.2c's shape and timing at ${\sim }1.5\times $ the amplitude, and the static run is drawn at zero---a spherical collapse radiates no quadrupole; its measured record stays below $3\times 10^{-8}$. (c)~The radiated energy $E_{20}=\tfrac  {1}{16\pi }\DOTSI \intop \ilimits@ |\DOTSI \intop \ilimits@ r\psi _4\,dt'|^{2}\,dt$ over the aligned burst, per unit $M_{\rm  ADM}$, at $R=18$ (filled) and $R=14$ (open): $3.3\times 10^{-7}\to 8.0\times 10^{-7}$ from the 0.2c to the 0.30c, a factor 2.5--3---the square of the amplitude ratio in (b)---with the static at zero. (d)~The speed of each extremum between neighbouring spheres, against the local coordinate speed of light (crosses): the burst propagates at light speed. The outcome: an unperturbed, axisymmetric rotating collapse radiates a quadrupole burst whose energy grows with spin as the amplitude squared, and the static collapse radiates nothing.}}{7}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:wave}{{5}{7}{The $(2,0)$ wave of the unperturbed rotating collapse. (a)~$r\,\Delta \psi _4^{20}$ of the 0.2c far-zone run on spheres $R=14$--45 against retarded time: one waveform with ring-down period ${\approx }24$; only the first lobe falls with $R$ (near field), the extrema are flat in $R$ to $1.5\%$ over $R=30$--45, and this box reproduces the $L=64$ run's throat history to $3.2\times 10^{-4}$. (b)~The wave against spin at $R=14$, each run on its own collapse clock $u=t-R-t_{10}$ ($t_{10}$: its equatorial deficit at $10\%$): the 0.30c burst has the 0.2c's shape and timing at ${\sim }1.5\times $ the amplitude, and the static run is drawn at zero---a spherical collapse radiates no quadrupole; its measured record stays below $3\times 10^{-8}$. (c)~The radiated energy $E_{20}=\tfrac {1}{16\pi }\int |\int r\psi _4\,dt'|^{2}\,dt$ over the aligned burst, per unit $M_{\rm ADM}$, at $R=18$ (filled) and $R=14$ (open): $3.3\times 10^{-7}\to 8.0\times 10^{-7}$ from the 0.2c to the 0.30c, a factor 2.5--3---the square of the amplitude ratio in (b)---with the static at zero. (d)~The speed of each extremum between neighbouring spheres, against the local coordinate speed of light (crosses): the burst propagates at light speed. The outcome: an unperturbed, axisymmetric rotating collapse radiates a quadrupole burst whose energy grows with spin as the amplitude squared, and the static collapse radiates nothing}{figure.5}{}}
